# Day 2 – Viewing and Searching File Content

## What I Learned
- Linux provides multiple commands to view file content.
- `less` is better than `cat` for large files.
- `head` and `tail` help preview beginning and end of files.
- `wc` counts lines, words and characters.
- `grep` is used to search text inside files (very important).
- `journalctl` for system logs.
## Commands Practiced

- less
  - `less practice.txt`
  - Used to scroll through file content.
  - Press `q` to exit.

- head
  - `head practice.txt`
  - Shows first 10 lines of a file.

- tail
  - `tail practice.txt`
  - Shows last 10 lines of a file.

- wc
  - `wc practice.txt`
  - Counts lines, words and characters.

- grep
  - `grep "Linux" practice.txt`
  - Searches for a word inside a file.
- journalctl
  - `journalctl -f` fro live logs.
  - `journalctl` for full view
  - `journalctl -n 20 | grep "failed" ` to search latest 20 logs for failed.

## What I Understood in Simple Words
These commands help us read and search inside files quickly without opening a text editor. This is very useful for reading logs and debugging systems.

## Problems I Faced
- Tried using `/var/log/syslog` but it showed file directory doesnot exist.

## How I Fixed Them
- Learned that Kali uses `journalctl` instead of syslog.
- Used: `journalctl | grep "failed"`
